 "about": "Cases for WordFixes.apply, the reader's own word fixes applied to a caption line, read by both OzenKitTests (WordFixesSharedCasesTests, Swift) and core WordFixesSharedCasesTest (Kotlin). fixes is the list as saved, each [heard, written], normalized before it is applied; expected is the line afterwards. Every case where a fix applies is here, and the ones where a heard word appears but must not change (a prefix the word may not take, a longer word, another case). Text joined by a maqaf (U+05BE) is in: both phones split words there as at a space, and a heard phrase matches only words joined the same way, by a space or by a maqaf. A fix whose written form the line already reads there (case, niqqud and maqaf against hyphen aside) leaves those words alone. The line may read it from the heard words or from a word before them (Avi Cohen, for a fix of cohen), at that word's first letter or past Hebrew prefix letters, and must read it on through the end of the heard words. It may lack a punctuation mark of the written form there (Dr Cohen, for a fix of cohen written Dr. Cohen), but not between the letters of the heard words, where the mark is what the fix changes (dont written don't), nor for a fix that only respells its words. A fix heard as Chinese characters (Japanese kanji too), which also matches inside a word, leaves the heard characters alone where the line already reads its written form around them, before, after or both, and may start and stop inside a word there (北京 or 北京市, for a fix of 京 written 北京). A fix heard in Japanese kana is also matched inside a word when it holds a kanji or a katakana letter (トム, ジョン・スミス, トム君, 田中さん): where it begins or ends in katakana, the katakana around it must end there, the long-vowel mark ー and small kana being katakana too, so トム is found in トムさんが and ジョン・スミス but not in トムソン or アトム, nor メアリ in メアリー. Such a fix also leaves its heard word alone where it stands as a word of its own and the line reads the written form from it on into the next word with no space there (トム ハンクスが, for トム written トム ハンクス; 王 医生来了), unless that reading stops inside a katakana run (トム ハンクスソン is another name). A heard word all in hiragana (a particle like は, が or の, or かった, the ending of 高かった) is still matched only as a whole word between spaces. A geresh, or an apostrophe standing in for one, right after the last Hebrew letter of a word belongs to it (George spelt in Hebrew ends in one): a fix replaces it with the rest, a heard word is found with or without it, and a heard phrase goes on past it to the next word, as it does not past a comma or a full stop; after any other letter it stays, as other punctuation does. A single quote that opens before a word's first letter (‘ or a straight ') is closed by the first matching mark after the last letter of that word or a later one: there it is the closing quote, not a geresh, and stays when a fix replaces the word, unless another apostrophe-like mark follows it (then that one closes the quote). A Hebrew geresh (U+05F3) is never a quote.",
